"""
Event-driven peak sampler: the output latches the value of `u` at every
local extremum, i.e. at each zero crossing of `der(u)`, using the clocked
sublanguage from `DiscreteComponents` (a `ZeroCrossingClock` driving a
`Sampler`, held back to continuous time by a `ZeroOrderHold`). This is the
strict version of the upstream `ShipSIM.Components.DataProcessing.PeakSampler`;
`PeakSampler` is the continuous approximation.

Read the sampled series from a solution through the clocked signal,
`sol[m.sampler.sampler.y]` (one entry per extremum); the held continuous
output `y` is stored as a discrete parameter timeseries that symbolic
indexing of the continuous solution does not reconstruct. The derivative
filter time `T_slope` sets how sharply the turning point is located.
"""
component EventPeakSampler
  u = RealInput()
  y = RealOutput()
  "Time constant of the derivative filter that locates the turning points"
  parameter T_slope::Time = 0.01
  slope = BlockComponents.Continuous.Derivative(k = 1, T = T_slope)
  clock = DiscreteComponents.ZeroCrossingClock()
  sampler = DiscreteComponents.Sampler()
  hold = DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold()
relations
  connect(u, slope.u)
  connect(slope.y, clock.u)
  connect(u, sampler.u)
  connect(clock.y, sampler.y)
  connect(sampler.y, hold.u)
  connect(hold.y, y)
end

"""
Drive `EventPeakSampler` with a chirp: the output must step to each new peak
value exactly when the input turns around.
"""
test component EventPeakSamplerChirp
  src = BlockComponents.Sources.Sine(amplitude = 5, frequency = 0.5, offset = 1)
  sampler = EventPeakSampler()
relations
  connect(src.y, sampler.u)
end

analysis EventPeakSamplerTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 10.0)
  model = EventPeakSamplerChirp()
end
